{"name":"Nightly Walk","hex":"#544563","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nightly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Happening or appearing in the night; night-time; nocturnal.","Performing, occurring, or taking place every night."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Every night."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nightly"},{"term":"walk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move on the feet by alternately setting each foot (or pair or group of feet: in the case of some non-human animals with four or more feet) forward, with at least one foot on the ground at all times. Compare run.","To \"walk free\", i.e. to win, or avoid, a criminal court case, particularly when actually guilty."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A trip made by walking.","A distance walked."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/walk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Walking","blocks":[{"text":"Walking is one of the main gaits of terrestrial locomotion among legged animals. Walking is typically slower than running and other gaits. Walking is defined as an \"inverted pendulum\" gait in which the body vaults over the stiff limb or limbs with each step. This applies regardless of the usable number of limbs—even arthropods, with six, eight, or more limbs, walk. In humans, limited research indicates that walking may be associated with reduced risk of diseases and premature death."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Women_walking_in_Hyderabad_street.jpg/330px-Women_walking_in_Hyderabad_street.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walking","wikidata":"Q6537379"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/1987WorldCupTrials.jpg/330px-1987WorldCupTrials.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/1987WorldCupTrials.jpg/330px-1987WorldCupTrials.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Racewalkers at the World Cup Trials in 1987","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1987WorldCupTrials.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Walking_gait_cycle.png/500px-Walking_gait_cycle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Walking_gait_cycle.png/500px-Walking_gait_cycle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Walking gait cycle starting with the left leg demonstrated. The loading cycle is where foot pronation naturally occurs.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWalking_gait_cycle.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Muybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif/330px-Muybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Muybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif/330px-Muybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Human walking cycle animation by Eadweard Muybridge","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/CGI_Human_Walk.jpg/500px-CGI_Human_Walk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/CGI_Human_Walk.jpg/500px-CGI_Human_Walk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Computer simulation of a human walk cycle. In this model the head keeps the same level at all times, whereas the hip follows a sine curve.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACGI_Human_Walk.jpg"},